DAY 11 - PART A: THEORY
Deployments and Rolling Updates
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. WHAT A DEPLOYMENT ADDS
-------------------------
Deployment = ReplicaSet + versioned updates.

  Deployment  (you manage this)
     |
     +-- ReplicaSet v1  (old template)   0 pods
     +-- ReplicaSet v2  (new template)   3 pods
              |
              +-- pods

When you change the pod template (image, env, resources ...), the
Deployment creates a NEW ReplicaSet and gradually moves pods from the
old one to the new one. The old ReplicaSet is kept (with 0 replicas)
so you can roll back.

Deployments are the standard way to run stateless apps.


2. DEPLOYMENT YAML
------------------
  apiVersion: apps/v1
  kind: Deployment
  metadata:
    name: web
  spec:
    replicas: 3
    selector:
      matchLabels:
        app: web
    strategy:
      type: RollingUpdate
      rollingUpdate:
        maxSurge: 1          # up to 1 extra pod during update
        maxUnavailable: 0    # never go below 3 ready pods
    template:
      metadata:
        labels:
          app: web
      spec:
        containers:
        - name: web
          image: nginx:1.26

It looks exactly like a ReplicaSet plus "strategy".


3. UPDATE STRATEGIES
--------------------
RollingUpdate (default)
  Replace pods a few at a time. No downtime if the app has readiness
  checks (Week 5).
    maxSurge:        how many EXTRA pods allowed (number or %) default 25%
    maxUnavailable:  how many pods can be NOT ready  default 25%

  Example, replicas=4, maxSurge=1, maxUnavailable=0:
    4 old -> 4 old + 1 new -> 3 old + 1 new -> 3 old + 2 new -> ...

Recreate
  Kill ALL old pods, then start new ones. Downtime, but never two
  versions at once (useful when versions can't coexist, e.g. a DB
  schema change).


4. WHAT TRIGGERS A ROLLOUT?
---------------------------
Only changes to spec.template (pod template). Examples:
  k set image deploy/web web=nginx:1.27
  k set env deploy/web COLOR=blue
  editing the template in YAML and applying
  k rollout restart deploy/web   (adds an annotation -> new pods)

Changing replicas does NOT create a new revision; it just scales.


5. ROLLOUT COMMANDS
-------------------
  k rollout status deploy/web     wait and show progress
  k rollout history deploy/web    list revisions
  k rollout undo deploy/web       go back one revision (Day 12)
  k rollout pause / resume deploy/web


6. NAMES YOU WILL SEE
---------------------
  Deployment:  web
  ReplicaSet:  web-7c5ddbdf54       (hash of the pod template)
  Pod:         web-7c5ddbdf54-x8k2p
The hash is also stored as the label "pod-template-hash".


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Deployment manages ReplicaSets; ReplicaSets manage pods.
- Template change = new ReplicaSet = rolling update.
- maxSurge / maxUnavailable control speed and safety.
- Recreate = downtime but no mixed versions.
